d3d12: scan out through a winsys displaytarget

Against mesa-26.2.0 (9f0a761020bca92f2b07156a0621e5360cb8eca5), the commit
payload.spec.json pins. Not submitted upstream.

A GPU-PV guest has exactly one DRM device, `vmlord_drm`, and it has no render
half -- so gbm loads the kms_swrast screen and `GALLIUM_DRIVER` picks what draws
into it. d3d12 draws there today: a context comes up on the host's adapter and
`eglSwapBuffers` succeeds. What did not work is the last hop, handing the frame
to KMS, and that is the whole of what this patch changes.

Two things were in the way.

The frame was never in a buffer KMS could address. gbm asks for a scanout buffer
and then asks that buffer for a DRM handle; d3d12 returned a resource in D3D12
memory, so the handle came back 0 and `drmModeAddFB2` answered EINVAL. llvmpipe
on the same device answers with handle 1, because it routes a `PIPE_BIND_SCANOUT`
resource onto a winsys displaytarget -- a dumb BO on the KMS device, which does
carry a handle. This patch does the same, and copies the rendered frame into that
displaytarget on `flush_resource`, which the frontend calls before every swap.
Both halves already existed in d3d12: displaytarget allocation through the winsys
and the copy in `d3d12_flush_frontbuffer`. Only `PIPE_BIND_DISPLAY_TARGET` reached
them, and gbm asks for `PIPE_BIND_SCANOUT`.

And with `GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT` the allocation failed outright, before any of that:
`PIPE_BIND_SCANOUT` forced `D3D12_TEXTURE_LAYOUT_ROW_MAJOR`, which D3D12 refuses
for a committed texture. A resource presented by copying does not need a row-major
layout -- the copy is what makes the layout linear -- so the demand is dropped
whenever there is a winsys to copy into. Without one, nothing here changes.

The cost is one full-frame readback per presented frame: a fixed cost in exchange
for a variable one. Measured on an RTX 5070 Ti through GPU-PV at 1080p, drawing N
blended full-screen passes, d3d12 against llvmpipe: 7.75/4.19 ms at N=0,
7.48/8.58 at N=2, 8.44/28.40 at N=8, 8.18/56.92 at N=16. d3d12 is flat because
what it pays is the copy; the two cross at about two passes, which a desktop
exceeds before it has drawn a window.

The floor is proportional to pixels alone -- 3.73 ms at 720p to 27.81 ms at
2160p -- 0.96 GiB/s at 1080p, against 5.64 GiB/s for a system-memory read of the
same frame in the same probe. Where that 5.9x goes is not established: the read
is staged through a READBACK heap, so a GPU copy, a fence wait and a CPU copy are
all in it, and which one dominates has not been measured. On at least one Intel
iGPU the same copy is pathological -- WSLg issue #1498 reports 327-359 ms per
frame at 720p -- so a host that is not known good deserves a measurement before
this path is trusted.

Refs: #180
